However, the last two lines are not necessary because context and detail are not dynamically allocated. The code snippet above will not compile if they are dynamically allocated.

Here's the corrected version:

```c
void report_error(const char *context, const char *detail) {
    char msg[MAX_MSG];
    snprintf(msg, MAX_MSG - 1, "%s: %s", context, detail);
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", msg);
    fflush(stderr);
}
```

If you want to handle dynamic memory, you should allocate it and free it correctly:

```c
void report_error(const char *context, const char *detail) {
    char *msg = malloc(MAX_MSG * sizeof(char));
    if (msg == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Memory allocation failed\n");
        return;
    }
    snprintf(msg, MAX_MSG - 1, "%s: %s", context, detail);
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", msg);
    fflush(stderr);
    free(msg);
}
