```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void send_response(FILE *stream, const char *response) {
    if (stream == NULL || response == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Invalid input parameters.\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    if (response == NULL) {
        fprintf(stream, "No response available.\n");
        return;
    }

    size_t response_len = strlen(response);
    if (response_len > 1024) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Response too long.\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    fprintf(stream, "%s", response);
    fflush(stream);
}
```

This code includes the following defensive programming practices:

- Input validation: It checks for `NULL` input parameters and handles them accordingly.
- Input bounds checking: It checks the length of the `response` string to prevent buffer overflow attacks.
- Safe string operations: It uses `fprintf` instead of `sprintf` to prevent format string vulnerabilities.

